From f2b6420011f2bfb90883081a7cc0aabec06760da Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jilongw <jilong.wang@intel.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 11:25:55 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 16/24] Add direct-kernel fused-K/V quant/dequant path for
 attention chunks

Fused-K/V attention chunks previously always went through split-heads
+ per-layer quantize (encode) and dequantize + head-merge (decode).
Add kvweave_serialize_fused_chunk_direct / kvweave_dequantize_fused_chunk_direct
support so per_channel/per_token scaling can skip that intermediate
split/merge step when the native build provides the direct kernels.

- kvweave_config: add LMCACHE_MP_KVWEAVE_FUSED_DIRECT_QUANT/DEQUANT env
  flags (default on).
- kvweave_serde: gate direct-quant on CPU and XPU via
  supports_fused_direct_quant(); gate direct-dequant to CPU only for
  now, since benchmarking (tests/benchmarks/bench_kvweave_fused_direct_quant.py)
  showed the XPU direct-dequant path is slower than dequant + device
  permute for per_channel and roughly a wash for per_token. Also wire
  up in-place serialize/dequantize-into paths (XPU DMA, CPU direct) so
  encode_chunk_into can write straight into the destination SHM slot
  without an intermediate bytes object. Cache the shared
  KVWeaveCodecConfig instance for Mamba precond tensors so repeated
  calls hit the native precond cache (keyed by the perm tensor's
  data_ptr) instead of missing it on every call with a fresh config.
- Add the attention-path precond (P/D) header bit and signs/perm
  plumbing for rh+precond serialize/deserialize, matching the existing
  Mamba path.
- Add a manual benchmark script comparing the direct kernel path
  against the existing split/merge path across devices, dtypes,
  scaling methods and quant configs.
- Add a regression test asserting the direct and split encode paths
  produce byte-identical payloads and matching decode error, and a
  unit test for LMCacheMPConnector.wait_for_save waiting on submitted
  store futures.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
